  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The basic character of hydroxides decreases from <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><msub><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> to <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><msub><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math>. Due to smaller size of <math> <mrow> <mtext>Lu,</mtext></mrow> </math> the <math> <mrow> <mtext>Lu</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>OH</mtext></mrow> </math> bond attains more covalent character.<br />Also, due to Lanthanide contraction <math> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mtext>Zr</mtext></mrow> <mrow> <mtext>4+</mtext></mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math> and <math> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mtext>Hf</mtext></mrow> <mrow> <mtext>4+</mtext></mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math> possess almost the same ionic radii.<br /><math> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mtext>Ce</mtext></mrow> <mrow> <mtext>4+</mtext></mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math> is a strong oxidant.<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/41995_1.JPG\" style=\"width: 304px; height: 86px;\" />",
    "image": null
